WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.560
Quantization stores model values at reduced precision to lower memory use and often

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.880
improve practical speed. Labels such as Q4, Q5, and Q8 describe broad precision families,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.560
though exact methods vary.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:15.945
Lower precision fits smaller hardware but can affect output quality differently across

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:19.180
models. Higher precision is not automatically better in an operational test.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.720
The referenced benchmark observed that Q8 did not always outperform Q4 for every model.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.750
A larger quantization may consume more memory, reduce headroom,

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.600
and trigger slower offloading without delivering a meaningful quality gain.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.750
Image-token budgets and preprocessing settings affect how much visual detail reaches the

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.040
model. Too little visual capacity may lose small text or objects.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.120
More tokens increase memory and latency and may not help if the source image is poor.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.270
Batch size, prompt-processing settings, context length, GPU offload, thread count,

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.518
and cache choices also change performance.

00:00:48.518 --> 00:00:52.360
Compare models under documented settings rather than treating the model name as the whole

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.328
configuration. Local runners such as llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio,

00:00:56.328 --> 00:01:00.630
and other tools may use different backends, model files, prompt templates, defaults,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:04.535
and vision adapters. Runtime version can alter speed, compatibility, and output.

00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:07.990
Test a small matrix of sensible settings, record memory and completion,

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.560
and keep one reproducible configuration per candidate.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.515
Choose the lowest-cost setting that meets task quality and stability rather than assuming

00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:15.880
the largest file is safest.
